    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d447">

                <pb facs="0426" n="418" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg418"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.0146/844: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The First Secretary of Embassy in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Smyth</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-08-28T15:00:00+09:00">August 28, 1940—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 28—11:46 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>291. Peiping’s 194, June 12, 4 p.m., and despatch No. 2702 of June 14,<anchor
                        n="47" xml:id="d447-f1"/> agreement maintenance <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >status quo</hi> Diplomatic Quarter, Peiping. The Commandants of the French
                    and Italian Embassy guards signed yesterday a supplementary agreement as
                    follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“In view of the French-Italian armistice and the withdrawal of British
                            troops from China the undersigned consider it advisable and do hereby
                            agree to revoke that portion of reference (<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >a</hi>) relating to liberty on alternate days and that henceforth
                            liberty and liberty areas will be as prescribed by the guard commanders
                            concerned.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Colonel Turnage, American and Senior Commandant who witnessed the signature of
                    the supplementary agreement, stated that it is designed <pb facs="0427" n="419"
                        xml:id="pg419"/> primarily to afford more liberty to the French and Italian
                    guard personnel.</p>
                <p>Present strength of Embassy guards in Peiping follow[s]: French, 2 officers and
                    32 men including 17 Annamites; Italian, 1 officer and 36 men; British, 9
                    enlisted men, chiefly signallers who do not wear military uniform.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department. Repeated to Chungking, Tientsin, Shanghai. By air mail to
                    Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smyth</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
